-- title:
--   Vanishing of the Fricke-transformed reduction on the 0-line
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $A$ a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, and $\mathrm{red}\colon A \to k$ a ring homomorphism. Let $a \in k$ lie in `ssJSet q k`, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $k$ with $j$-invariant $a$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$, and suppose $a^{q^2} = a$. Let $f$ be an element of the field `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $1 \cdot q$ inside Laurent series. Assume the Laurent expansions of $f$ and of its image under `frickeInvolutionBar (1 * q)` both lie in the ring `CharPReduction.modularLocalized (1 * q) A.toSubring red` (the modular ring of level $1\cdot q$, localised at the kernel of $\mathrm{red}$), and that their images under the coefficientwise reduction `CharPReduction.modularRedLocHom (1 * q) A.toSubring red` both lie in `modularFunctionFieldC k 1`, the subfield of Laurent series over $k$ generated by the reductions of $j$ and of $j$ at level $1$. Assume further that no pole of $f$ is centred at the supersingular crossing over $a$: for every place $W$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $W.\mathrm{ord}(f) < 0$, it fails that simultaneously some $x \in A$ with $\mathrm{red}(x) = a$ satisfies $W.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath - x) > 0$ and some $y \in A$ with $\mathrm{red}(y) = a^q$ satisfies $W.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath_q - y) > 0$, where $\bar\jmath$ and $\bar\jmath_q$ denote the elements of the field given by the $q$-expansion `jq` and by its expansion `qExpand ℚ (1 * q) jq`. Finally assume that the reduction of $f$ has value $0$ at the first place of the pair `frobNodePair q a` (it lies in that valuation subring and its residue is $0$), and that the reduction of the Fricke transform has non-negative order at every place of `modularFunctionFieldC k 1` over $k$. Then that reduction of the Fricke transform is $0$.
--
--   In the geometric picture of the special fibre of $X_0(q)$ at $q$ as two rational curves meeting at the supersingular points, this says that a function which is integral along both branches, vanishes at the node over $a$ read on the $\infty$-branch, and has no pole on the $0$-branch, vanishes identically on the $0$-branch. It is used in the analysis of residues of functions whose poles are concentrated at the cusp $\bar\infty$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_modularRedLocHom_frickeInvolutionBar_eq_zero_of_hasValue_zero_of_forall_ord_nonneg.lean

